Date: Wed, 10 Feb 93 09:06:30 EST
From: Blair Haworth <wbh@mail.lib.duke.edu>
Subject: HIST: This Too Shall Pass...

          Perry Metzger writes:

          >>From: Duncan Frissell <76630.3577@compuserve.com>

          >>[...]  The nation state was invented
          >>about 1600 AD.  It is not written in stone.  The Church
          >>used to be a major power center in Europe.  It no longer
          >>is.  That revolution occurred with little bloodshed.

          >Here I must disagree with Duncan's historical facts.

          I'm going to have to work out on yours a bit.

          >The nation-state long predates 1600AD, although several
          >modern European states started up back then. Certainly the
          >Roman Empire was a huge state.  Certainly England was a
          >nation-state as early as 1066AD. Certainly China has been
          >run and organized on nation-state lines for nearly 2000
          >years.

          Wow.  This involves a pretty simplistic definition of the
          nation-state, more or less encompassing what farmers
          denounce as "th'gummint" between subsidy checks.  While the
          definition of the nation-state is mutable and controversial,
          it revolves around a government having a tenable
          claim of sovereignty, national identity and a monopoly of
          violence, normally with an extensive economic.  None of
          these really fits.  The Roman Empire's governmental
          institutions were fundamentally personal; this is what made
          _every_ Caesar's succession such a trauma, even the
          Antonines.  The Republic was closer to a nation-state, but
          not identical.  It certainly wasn't the paradigm they strted
          from.

          Similarly, England wasn't a nation-state in 1066; it was
          booty, being transferred from the grip of a bunch of
          imperfectly anglicized Vikings to a bunch of imperfectly
          frenchified Vikings at the end of a long turf war with
          another bunch of Vikings from the Old Country.  As such, it
          was an enormous personal estate run by a pyramid of armed
          sharecroppers.  The formal details of high feudalism are a
          later overlay.  And even that formal structure excluded
          modern ideas of sovereignty.

          In the interests of bandwidth, I won't even begin to attack
          the notion of a multimillennial, unitary "China", save to
          say that its political theory was, as near as I can say,
          personal rather than national.

          >The church's hold on power was not broken bloodlessly,
          >either. The Thirty Years War, which ended with the Treaty
          >of Westphalia in 1648, was one of the bloodiest messes on
          >record. The Thirty Years War was essentially about whether
          >the Catholic Church was to continue to rule Europe. It lost
          >-- one of the major conditions of the treaty was the
          >subordination of national established religions to the
          >power of the local state.

          This is simplistic, too.  While the Thirty Years' War
          started on a confessional basis, the Treaty of Westphalia
          was a political phenomenon, as was the last half of the war
          itself (it's reaching to refer to the TYW as a unitary
          event, but that's another lecture).  Even the confessional
          element was not so much about the continued "rule" (another
          complex anachronism) of the Roman Church, but whether it
          would make a comeback.  The principle of a unitary Western
          Christendom had been quietly smothered as a political basis
          for Europe at the conclusion of the earlier set of religious
          wars.  It was replaced by the trade unionism of princes
          expressed in the principle _cuius regio, eius religio_ that
          you refer to above.  The Treaty of Westphalia essentially
          upheld the (pro-nation-state) _status quo ante bellum_,
          fossilizing it until the French Revolution.

          Sorry about the length of this reply, but it could easily
          have been longer.  No one's going to get anywhere (save
          Bedlam) by citing such faulty datain a reasonably serious
          discussion.  History is built on mounds of faulty data, of
          course, but there's no reason to exacerbate the problem.

          --Blair Haworth


