Date: Wed, 10 Feb 93 09:13:25 CST
From: twb3@midway.uchicago.edu (Tom Morrow)
Subject: POLI: married to the State

David Friedman has a really neat description of marriage in his PRICE
THEORY (Cinncinnati, South-Western Publ. Co.: 1986) 484-488.  (I highly
recommend this book, and point out that there is a more recent edition.) 
He describes the marriage relationship as a bilateral monopoly with a lot
of firm specific capital.  Lately I have been thinking of the relationship
between statists and their subjects in somewhat the same way.

Statist institutions tend to have a lot of firm specific capital.  They
cannot, for example, easily convert defense services to other productive
functions.  Statists' subjects also often have heavy investments in the
political infrastructure.  They cannot, for example, easily transfer their
property and allegiances to other statist institutions.  They can emigrate,
but only if they are willing to bear huge transaction costs.  Statists and
their subjects are thus in something like a bilateral monopoly with firm
specific capital, and each thus needs assurances that the other will not
try to use their assets as a bargaining lever.  The solution, here as in
marriage, is a long term contract.  This may help us to understand the
appeal of constitutions.

Please understand that I am not saying that statism and marriage are
*identical*!  Clearly the contract with statists with a much greater
imbalance in power.  Statists need loyal subjects, but they can push them
around a great deal.  This may explain why many constitutions at least
appear to put greater limits on the behavior of statists than on their
subjects; it is an effort to balance the power between statists and their
subjects.  That is the way the US Constitution was initially intended to
work, at least.  But the fact that much of the rhetoric supporting the
theory of the "social contract" is just that--only rhetoric--shows that the
bilateral monopoly analysis fits only imperfectly.  Still, it may tell us
how to divorce ourselves from statists.

Friedman's analysis goes on to consider why marriages have become less
stable, and concludes that specialization in labor is the major cause. 
Services once available only through marriages (childcare, food production,
cleaning, etc.) are increasing available through market channels.  The
costs of not being married have thus dropped, and the single life thus
become more attractive.  

Perhaps the same may become true of the statist/subject relationship as
entrepreneurs figure out more and more ways to coopt statist services. 
(See, eg, FedEx and private security companies.)  The free flow of capital
across statist boundaries will also push this trend.  Both factors make it
less expensive to forego political allegiances.  

This analysis also tells us that we ought to try to make it less expensive
for statists to give up their side of the bargain if we want to convince
them to let us go peaceably.  Regardless of how you view the ethics of
"buying off the enemey", it has been used with some success in places
recently freed of Soviet control.  Here in the US a similar policy would
allow statists to become owners and operators of the services they now hold
as civil servants.  Air traffic controllers, for example, will be more
likely to support less government if they are assured that they will be
allowed to hold onto their radars and compete with newcomers.


