Date: Wed, 10 Feb 93 10:18:15 EST
From: Andrew S Hall <ashall@magnus.acs.ohio-state.edu>
Subject: ECON:An interesting case

On the new this AM I heard about a judge in Michigan who told GM
that they can't close a plant because it would be detrimental to
the community. Immediately this sounded like the usual crypto-
commie garbage. However, he gave his rationale as being that
GM executives made what was essentially a verbal contract to the
town (presumably to keep the plant running) in exchange to huge
tax breaks. This raised some questions for me.

Leaving aside the stupidity of wanting to tax a company that is
bringing jobs to your community and assuming that such a promise
was made by the executives:

- Is the judge correct in enforcing such an oral contract? Even
  though the result is basically state controlled economy, the
  executives are guilty of a breach of contract.
- Should the judge (acting as independant arbitrator) have provided
  some other penalty such as requiring GM to pay the taxes that it
  was recused from? This would seem to be a logical punishment for
  breach of contract.
- Is there no breach of contract since the excutives that made the
  promise probably had no authority to do so and the town shouldn't
  been so stupid as to believe the promise anyway?

I think this is a nice test case of results vs. principles. The results
are cruddy, but may be the only way to maintain the principles.


Andrew Hall

