Date: Wed, 10 Feb 93 09:35:45 CST
From: twb3@midway.uchicago.edu (Tom Morrow)
Subject: POLI: Collectivist justification

Libertarians tend to piss a lot of people off when they start to claim that
statism is not justified.  I think I know why, and the explanation will
surprise you.  Libertarians are not only *wrong* but *collectivist* when
they make such broad claims about justification.

Why are they wrong?  Because statism is not universally unjustified.  I
have outlined my theory of political justification in prior posts. 
Briefly, I argue that justification is relative only to individuals (or
other entities capable of giving consent, such as an institution all of
whose individual members have consented to let represent their interests). 
This means that I can only claim that, for example, the US is not justified
*relative to me*.  I cannot speak for my neighbor.

I have little doubt that if the matter were put to a public referendum
today most Americans would expressly consent to the jurisdiction of the US
Constitution.  That you would not does not preclude *them* from doing so,
and they quite rightfully object to claims that the US Constitution cannot
be justified *at all*.  Libertarians who imply otherwise are actually using
a collectivist argument: "Because I do not consent to statism, nobody else
can either."

Having once made similar claims I now attribute to some libertarians, I
fully understand the appeal of claiming that statism is not justified.  But
while I agree with the spirit of that claim, I feel that accuracy and
diplomacy require a different approach.  I now try to say, "Statism is not
justified relative to me, because I refuse to give my express consent.  But
because I recognize each individual's right to decide whether or not
statism is justifed relative to him or her, I cannot decide whether statism
is justified *to you*.  You can choose to live under statism, and indeed
many people do.  For reasons I will be happy to outline, I think that this
it is unwise and unecessary to accept statism.  But I will respect your
choice do so.  I ask only that you in turn respect my right to deny my
allegiance to the institutions you and other statists agree to live under."

Some folks argue that by denying statists the right to rule over me, I am
effectively strippling statism of all content.  I think this inaccurate,
though it does point to an interesting effect of my argument.  I've said
enough for now, though.  I'll respond to further inquiry should it arise. 


