>- Is the judge correct in enforcing such an oral contract? 

Generally speaking, an oral contract that is not to be performed within one
year of its creation is not enforceable.  See the statute of frauds.  This
would appear to apply to the GM case, but there must have been mitigating
factors such as state legislation.  

>- Should the judge (acting as independant arbitrator) have provided
>  some other penalty such as requiring GM to pay the taxes that it
>  was recused from? This would seem to be a logical punishment for
>  breach of contract.

Yes, remedies are usually limited to monetary damages.  "Specific
performance," ie, making the breaching party carry through on the contract,
is only enforced in extraordinary cases.  Maybe the judge found the GM case
to be extraordinary.  It does seem to be true that merely paying back taxes
would not give the city the full benefit of its bargain, since it had
planned to have GM support a community of workers.  It would be more
appropriate, though still imperfect, to force GM to compensate the workers
for lost wages or the costs of moving to new jobs.  But it may be that
judge only issued an injunction to force GM to stay until damages could be
set and paid.  

>- Is there no breach of contract since the excutives that made the
>  promise probably had no authority to do so and the town shouldn't
>  been so stupid as to believe the promise anyway?

Maybe.  It depends on the facts.  I'll wager that if the judge considered
this defense that he or she decided the city officials acted reasonably in
treating GM's agents as having the apparent authority to make the deal.

You are right, this is an interesting case.  Communtarians are going to be
chewing on it for a long while.

