Date:    Wed, 10 Feb 93 11:20 EST
From: "Andrew I Cohen"                            <UANDCOH@uncmvs.oit.unc.edu>
Subject: Wills vs. slavery contracts

Recently, Mark Read Pickens posted a message about the possibility
of slavery contracts:
>
> >> Can people sell themselves into slavery?  Slaves, by definition,
> >> are property.  Property has no right to own, let alone sell,
> >> anything.  At the moment the prospective slave would have become
> >> property, he lost the right to complete the transaction.  It seems
> >> to me we have a logical contradiction.
>
> >> Mark Read Pickens
>
> > Corporations are property, and they can buy and sell (they even buy
> > other corporations).  You might object that they do so only as
> > agents of their owners, but why shouldn't that theory apply to a
> > slave?
>
> > If a transaction is invalid because at some point one of the parties
> > becomes incompetent, then I can't hire you to do something in the
> > event of my death--you have no reason to do it, because the dead
> > can't make contracts.
>
> > Anton Sherwood
>
> True, dead people can't make contracts, however the agreement is valid
> because we finalized it while you were alive and presumably competent.
> Slaves, in contrast, would become incompetent before the contract could be
> completed, which would invalidate it.
>
> Mark Read Pickens   autarch@well.sf.ca.us

I don't think this quite captures the oddity of slavery contracts and
how they differ from contracts fulfilled contingent on my death.

I'd peg the difference in the "enforceability" of the contracts.
In the case of a will, or some other such contract, Jones (the
soon-to-be-dead) has a cluster of rights he is transferring to
Smith when he (Jones) dies.  When Jones dies, Jones is not around
to uphold the contract.  But _Smith_ is.  Jones existed long enough
with the moral and legal standing to transfer some rights to Smith.
After that, it is Smith, the beneficiary of the will, who can
insist on the terms of the contract against everyone else in
the world (particularly unruly family members of Jones's).

Death doesn't invalidate wills because it is irrelevant to the
substance of the will.  It is merely a condition for someone mentioned
in the will to get some new rights.

In the case of slavery, this is different.  If Jones allegedly
contracts with Smith to become his slave, then, after the surrender
of all moral and legal standing, who gets to "stand on" the terms
of the contract?  The slaveholder can insist on its terms, but not
against the slave (only against the rest of the world).  Consider
this, however:  what if the slaveholder "reneges" on his end of
the contract?  In other words, what if the slaveholder gets slack
in being a slaveholder?  Can the slave "stand on" the terms of the
slavery contract?  Nope; he's lost all moral and legal standing with
which to do so.  At best, a slavery contract is thus a completely
revocable "gift" from the slaveholder-to-be to the slave-to-be.
It's not a full-fledged contract because one of the "beneficiaries"
of the exchange loses the standing upon which to insist on the
terms of the exchange after the fact.

Although. . . hmm.  I can see some objections:  the "dead" party
is a beneficiary to the will in the same sense the slave-to-be
is a beneficiary of slave contracts.

Nah.  In the case of slavery contracts, the promised exchange is
ongoing.  But you can't have a contractual exchange if one of
the contractors doesn't have the standing on which to insist on
the terms of the exchange.  So I'd say that slavery contracts
are occcasions where two parties mumble some words generating
completely mutually revocable promises of future exchanges of goods
and services.

Would that do it?

