From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: Econ: Zero-sum games

I wrote:
> In theory, the only risk that the market
> will/should pay you for is beta.  The rest you can eliminate with
> enough diversification.  Diversify your bets.

Hal Finney responded:
>Now, Robin suggests that diversification is the key to eliminating the
>second risk.  One must ask, then, why bet at all?  If you take so many
>betting positions that you'll win as many as you lose, you may have
>diversified away your betting risk, but you've also diversified away
>any betting return.  You could have just held the underlying security
>if that was your goal. ...  What is maximal diversification in a
>betting market?  It is to hold both sides of the bet! ... This means,
>I think, that diversification in the sense used in CAPM doesn't
>really apply to a betting market.   

The same issue arises when considering to "bet" by investing in just a
few stocks one thinks are undervalued, or by buying a market index,
and is dealt with at length in standard finance texts.  The key is
that the marginal return to diversification is very low after you have
20-100 diverse assets in your portfolio.  By picking 100 diverse
favorites, you have almost as much diversification as you could get
with the full market, and can leverage your special beliefs about
where the market is wrong.  

The same holds when you add bets to your portfolio; you can sacrifice
just a little diversification to gain a lot of (personally) expected
return with 100 diverse bets.  

>P.S. According to my source, one reason why CAPM doesn't work for such
>markets is that it assumes that the quantity of each available asset
>is fixed.  But the number of betting contracts is highly elastic, ...
>New pairs of bets can be
>created without limit, without hurting the value of existing bets.

This is silly.  A similar argument would imply that baskets or index
funds invalidate CAPM, since baskets can be created at will from the
items they group.  You can think of ordinary assets as baskets of
contingent assets.

Robin Hanson

